<Layout
  title="How to Download YouTube Subtitles – Usage Guide"
  description="Step-by-step guide to downloading YouTube subtitles and transcripts with FetchSubs: copy the link, pick a language, export SRT, VTT, TXT or RAW."
>
<div class="doc-page">
    <h1>Usage Guide & Workflow</h1>
    
    <p>FetchSubs is designed to be the fastest way to get readable transcripts and subtitle files from YouTube videos. Here is how you can get the most out of our tool.</p>

    <h2>Step-by-Step Instructions</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Copy URL:</strong> Open YouTube and copy the link (URL) of the video you want subtitles for.</li>
        <li><strong>Paste & Fetch:</strong> Return to FetchSubs, paste the link into the search box, and hit the "Download" button.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose Format:</strong> Once the video details appear, browse through the available languages and select your preferred format.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Understanding the Formats</h2>
    <p>We provide four ways to access your subtitles:</p>
    
    <ul>
        <li><strong>SRT (SubRip):</strong> The standard subtitle format. Includes precise timecodes. Ideal for video editing or watching in media players like VLC.</li>
        <li><strong>VTT (WebVTT):</strong> The web standard for HTML5 video players. Same timecodes as SRT, ideal for websites, LMS platforms and players like Video.js. See <a href="/youtube-to-srt">YouTube to SRT &amp; VTT</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>TXT (Clean Text):</strong> Best for reading. We strip away all timecodes and metadata, giving you a clean, paragraph-style transcript. Perfect for studying or note-taking.</li>
        <li><strong>RAW (Quick View):</strong> Opens the clean text directly in your browser tab. No download required—just instant reading.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Typical Use Cases</h2>
    <p>FetchSubs is widely used by various professionals and students:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Students:</strong> Quickly convert lecture videos into readable study notes.</li>
        <li><strong>Content Creators:</strong> Analyze competitor videos or repurpose own video content into blog posts.</li>
        <li><strong>Language Learners:</strong> Download dual-language subtitles to study sentence structures and vocabulary.</li>
        <li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> Get transcripts for videos that lack proper descriptive captions.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

</Layout>
